From: nerves_anbernic <none@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: pwrseq_simple: fall back to GPIO reset when no reset controller

Since the reset-controller lookup was added to mmc_pwrseq_simple_probe(), a
node describing exactly one reset-gpios entry and no resets property fails to
probe:

    pwrseq_simple pwrseq: error -ENOENT: reset control not ready
    pwrseq_simple pwrseq: probe with driver pwrseq_simple failed with error -2

On the Anbernic RG40XXV that node powers the RTL8821CS SDIO WiFi. The failed
probe means mmc1 never initialises, so no SDIO device is discovered, no rtw88
module is autoloaded, and wlan0 never appears.

The driver already has a GPIO reset fallback immediately below, documented as
being for platforms without RESET support -- but the early return prevents it
from ever being reached. Treat a missing reset controller as "use the GPIO
path" and keep propagating -EPROBE_DEFER.

--- a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
@@ -137,9 +137,18 @@
 	ngpio = of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "reset-gpios", "#gpio-cells");
 	if (ngpio == 1) {
 		pwrseq->reset_ctrl = devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(dev, NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
-			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
-					     "reset control not ready\n");
+		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl)) {
+			if (PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl) == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+				return -EPROBE_DEFER;
+
+			/*
+			 * No reset controller for this node. That is not fatal:
+			 * fall through to the GPIO based reset handled below,
+			 * which is what boards describing reset-gpios without a
+			 * resets property rely on.
+			 */
+			pwrseq->reset_ctrl = NULL;
+		}
 	}
 
 	/*
